The '''1974 NFL draft''' took place at the [[Americana Hotel]] in [[New York City]], New York, on January 29–30, 1974.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.footballgeography.com/nfl-draft-sites/ |title=NFL Draft Locations |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |date=October 2, 2014 |website=FootballGeography.com |access-date=October 23, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150905090250/http://www.footballgeography.com/nfl-draft-sites/ |archive-date=September 5, 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Salomone |first1=Dan |title=NFL Draft headed to Chicago in 2015 |url=http://www.giants.com/news-and-blogs/article-1/NFL-Draft-headed-to-Chicago-in-2015/3ac322b1-3b4c-4a16-aba7-6e08d31b1756 |access-date=June 3, 2015 |publisher=[[New York Giants]] |work=Giants.com |date=October 2, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150930065327/http://www.giants.com/news-and-blogs/article-1/NFL-Draft-headed-to-Chicago-in-2015/3ac322b1-3b4c-4a16-aba7-6e08d31b1756 |archive-date=September 30, 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Each of the 26 NFL teams were granted 17 selections for a total of 442 picks.<ref>{{cite news|title=Noll Seeks First-Round Draft Ace No. 6|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=OpxRAAAAIBAJ&sjid=Am0DAAAAIBAJ&pg=1988%2C3223203|access-date=October 25, 2011|newspaper=[[Pittsburgh Post-Gazette]]|date=January 29, 1974}}</ref>

Many experts consider the [[1974 Pittsburgh Steelers season|Pittsburgh Steelers]] to have had the best draft class in NFL history as they selected four players later inducted into the [[Pro Football Hall of Fame]] ([[Lynn Swann]], [[Jack Lambert (American football)|Jack Lambert]], [[John Stallworth]], and [[Mike Webster]]). A fifth Hall of Famer, [[Donnie Shell]], was signed by Pittsburgh after going unselected in the draft.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nfl.com/news/pro-football-hall-of-fame-centennial-class-thick-with-nfl-histo-0ap3000001096808|title=Pro Football Hall of Fame Centennial Class thick with NFL history|first=Judy|last=Battista|website=NFL.com|date=January 15, 2020|access-date=January 15, 2020}}</ref> The closest any other team has come to this success in a draft is the [[1964 Dallas Cowboys season|Dallas Cowboys]]' [[1964 NFL draft|1964 draft]], when three Hall of Famers were taken ([[Roger Staubach]], [[Mel Renfro]], and [[Bob Hayes]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Archer |first1=Todd |title=Three Hall of Famers make 1964 Cowboys' best draft class |url=https://www.espn.com/blog/dallas-cowboys/post/_/id/4750530/three-hall-of-famers-make-1964-cowboys-best-draft-class |website=ESPN |date=25 April 2016 |access-date=27 January 2022}}</ref> The [[1958 Green Bay Packers season|Green Bay Packers]] also selected three Hall of Famers in [[1958 NFL Draft|1958]] ([[Jim Taylor (fullback)|Jim Taylor]], [[Ray Nitschke]], and [[Jerry Kramer]]).

The [[1974 Houston Oilers season|Houston Oilers]] had the first pick in the 1974 draft based on their one-win record in [[1973 Houston Oilers season|1973]], but they traded the [[List of first overall National Football League Draft picks|first overall pick]]—as well as the first pick of the third round, #53 overall—to the [[1974 Dallas Cowboys season|Dallas Cowboys]] in exchange for [[defensive end]] [[Tody Smith]] and [[wide receiver]] [[Billy Parks]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.dallascowboys.com/news/news.cfm?id=F979DBDA-E6DC-62DE-0692F849C18A9EA9|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090720064304/http://www.dallascowboys.com/news/news.cfm?id=F979DBDA-E6DC-62DE-0692F849C18A9EA9|title=Dallas Cowboys.com: Cowboys Top 50 List: No. 26 Ed Too Tall Jones|archivedate=July 20, 2009}}</ref> Dallas used the two picks to select two future Pro Bowlers, defensive end [[Ed "Too Tall" Jones]] and quarterback [[Danny White]].

This was the first NFL draft since [[1938 NFL draft|1938]] to not have any quarterbacks taken in the first round, and one of only six. Along with [[1988 NFL draft|1988]], it is the only draft where no quarterback was taken in the first two rounds, and 1974 is generally regarded as one of the worst quarterback draft classes of all time, with only Danny White (the first quarterback taken, with the first pick in the third round) and fourth round pick [[Mike Boryla]] reaching the Pro Bowl, and even Boryla was out of the NFL by 1978.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.eldo.co/5/post/2018/04/nfl-draft-history-quarterback-draft-class-facts-and-figures.html|title=NFL Draft History: Quarterback Draft Class Facts/Figures|website=ELDORADO}}</ref>

In the seventeenth round, the [[1974 Green Bay Packers season|Green Bay Packers]] selected wide receiver [[Randy Woodfield]] of [[Portland State Vikings football|Portland State]] (428th overall). Cut in training camp, he later became a convicted serial killer, known as the 'I-5 killer'.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.si.com/longform/true-crime/i-5-killer-green-bay-packers-randall-woodfield/index.html | title=Randall Woodfield, the I-5 killer, former Green Bay Packer | access-date=January 30, 2023 | archive-date=November 18, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181118141225/https://www.si.com/longform/true-crime/i-5-killer-green-bay-packers-randall-woodfield/index.html | url-status=dead }}</ref> 

The last remaining active player in the NFL from the 1974 draft class was center [[Mike Webster]], who retired after the [[1990 NFL season|1990 season]].

